    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-182247",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d4954fe5-f1fe-590a-9540-280a14606a49",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(P({x}))={\u2205,{\u2205},{{x}},{\u2205,{x}}}$\n- $P({{x}})={\u2205,{x}}$\n- $P({x,\u2205})={\u2205,{x},{\u2205},{x,\u2205}}$",
      "worked_solution": "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $\u2205.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the power set of the set ${x}$ is ${\u2205,{x}}.$ Therefore, $P(P({x}))={\u2205,{\u2205},{{x}},{\u2205,{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that we are given the set ${{x}}.$ Then, the power set of the set ${{x}}$ is ${\u2205,{{x}}}.$ Therefore, $P({{x}})={\u2205,{{x}}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that we are given the set ${x,\u2205}.$ So, its power set must be ${\u2205,{x},{\u2205},{x,\u2205}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
